slang: --relax-string-compare

"invalid operands to binary expression ('byte' and 'string')" (BadBinaryExpression) for a
string compared with an integral operand, as in `s[i] == t` (a byte of one string against
another string). IEEE 1800-2017 6.16 compares two strings, or a string with a string
literal; VCS and Xcelium accept it. The new CompilationFlags::RelaxStringCompare (driver
option --relax-string-compare) makes BinaryExpression::fromComponents convert the
integral operand of a comparison with a string to a string, as string'(x) would, and then
compare two strings. Found in OpenTitan's csrng and entropy_src DV. Applies on top of 0011
(same enum).

--- a/include/slang/ast/Compilation.h
+++ b/include/slang/ast/Compilation.h
@@ -157,9 +157,13 @@ enum class SLANG_EXPORT CompilationFlags {
     /// Allow unpacked arrays (fixed, dynamic, queue) to be assigned without a cast when
     /// their element types are integral with the same width and differ only in
     /// four-statedness or signedness (e.g. a logic [7:0] queue from a bit [7:0] queue).
-    RelaxArrayElementConversions = 1 << 23
+    RelaxArrayElementConversions = 1 << 23,
+
+    /// Allow a string to be compared with an integral operand (e.g. a byte of another
+    /// string); the integral operand is converted to a string, as by a cast.
+    RelaxStringCompare = 1 << 24
 };
-SLANG_BITMASK(CompilationFlags, RelaxArrayElementConversions)
+SLANG_BITMASK(CompilationFlags, RelaxStringCompare)
 
 /// Contains various options that can control compilation behavior.
 struct SLANG_EXPORT CompilationOptions {
